; Chuckie Egg (A&F Software): ULAX driver with the Spec256 colours.
;
; A .dux file fills ULAX memory for one snapshot (chuckiegg.sna / .szx). Format: docs/file_formats/ulax.md.
; It has two parts:
;   1. COMMANDS (addresses 10xxxx, below): one point-machine command per Z80 code address (xxxx is the
;      48K address). They run when the Z80 executes the instruction at that address, and move the colour
;      of sprite pixels along with the sprite data. They are copied unchanged from the ULAX driver
;      (chuckiegg.dux of 'Chuckie Egg'); this file does not decode each one.
;      C08D / C08E: enable colour tags and load the active colours from reference colours 8,D / 8,E.
;      C888: back to the standard Spectrum colours with tags disabled. 0130, 0131, 0113 and the others:
;      pixel copy commands (see the docs).
;   2. COLOUR DATA (addresses 80xxxx, after the commands): the colour of sprite bytes in the game's RAM.
;
; What differs from the ULAX driver is the colour data only. The ULAX driver picked its own colours;
; here each byte it colours takes its colour from the Spec256 version (chuckie.gfx + chuckie.sna, recoloured
; by Ville Torren), converted through the Spec256 palette to a ULAX direct colour GRB555 (0GGGGGRRRRRBBBBB).
; The same bytes are coloured as in the ULAX driver; every other pixel keeps the Spectrum attribute colours.
;
109308	0130
10930E	0213 0 0130
109315	0213
10934B	0133
1093A2	0130 0113	; CHANGED from 0131 0113, see below

1092FD	C08D
10935E	C888
10935F	C888
1093DC	C888


109A70	0111 0 0111	; CHANGED from 0100 0 0100, see below
109A86	5A11 0 0 5C11 0 0 5C11
109B11	0131 0931 0113	; CHANGED from 0131 0730 0113, see below
109B4E	0131 0113 0 0 0131 0113 0 0 0131 0113
109B93	0133

109A6E	C888	; CHANGED from C08E, see below
109A93	C888


; CHANGED and ADDED commands, for the corn pile (see the end of this file). The game draws a tile with an
; 8-byte copy loop (LD A,(HL) / LD (DE),A; the tile table is at 84F0). 0130 reads the tile byte's pixels into
; register 3, with the codes FFF0 to FFF7 replaced by the pixels of the translation register T (this needs tags
; on, see the bricks below), and 0113 writes them to the screen byte, so the colours of a tile byte travel with
; it, as in the Spec256 colour memory. Tiles whose bytes carry no colour copy as plain Spectrum pixels.
; ADDED 109C83: the loop at 9C80-9C87 draws most tiles to the screen. The loop at 93A0-93A6 does the same: its
;   pair 0131 0113 at 1093A2 (above) is CHANGED to 0130 0113. 0131 is the same read without the T codes.
109C83	0130 0113
; ADDED 109AD1 and 109AD3: the loop at 9AD1-9ADC copies the 3 x 3 tiles around Harry into a background buffer
;   at 72DD (LD A,(HL) at 9AD1, LD (IX+0),A at 9AD2-9AD4, whose last M1 is 9AD3).
109AD1	0130
109AD3	0113
; CHANGED 109B11 (above): 0131 0730 0113 became 0131 0931 0113. The loop at 9B11-9B16 draws Harry over that
;   background (LD A,(DE) / OR (HL) / LD (DE),A, DE = the sprite buffer 72A0, HL = the background). The
;   ULAX driver's 0730 is xmask with source register 0, the background byte in plain ink and paper, which drops
;   the background's colours: the pile turned magenta whenever Harry came near it and stayed so. 0931 is xback
;   with source register 1, the background pixels with their colours; where both Harry and the background have
;   a colour, Harry's pixel stays.
; CHANGED 109A70 and 109A6E: the routine at 9A4C copies Harry's frame into the sprite buffer (72A0) with two LDI
;   instructions (ED A0 at 9A6F and 9A71; 9A70 and 9A72 are the last M1 of each). The ULAX driver's 0100 writes
;   the byte in the active colours, which only its tags set, so it can give a byte one ink and one paper.
;   0111 (the cheat-sheet command for ldi) copies the pixels with their own colours instead, which is what
;   gives every pixel of Harry its Spec256 colour. C08E at 9A6E (tags on, active colours = reference colours 8
;   and E) became C888 (standard colours, tags off), so the padding byte the loop writes after each row stays
;   a plain paper pixel and is not turned into a coloured one.
;
; ADDED, for the bricks (platforms), whose colours change with the level (see the end of this file):
;   10A600 and 10A6F5: 5FF1 loads the translation register T from the pixels of the byte an LDIR has just read.
;      The two LDIR instructions that copy a level's map to 61A8 (A5FF at the start of the game, A6F4 at every
;      later level) end on the last byte of the map block, so T ends up as the pixels the dux gives that byte:
;      a colour scheme per level.
;   109C7D / 109C89, 109398 / 1093A8, 109ACF / 109ADE: C088 turns colour tags on (with the standard colours) just
;      before each of the three loops that copy a tile's bytes, and C888 turns them off just after, so that the
;      codes FFF0 to FFF7 in the pixels the loops read resolve through T (they only do with tags on) and nothing
;      else changes.
10A600	5FF1	; T = pixels of the map byte just copied (game start)
10A6F5	5FF1	; T = pixels of the map byte just copied (next level)
109C7D	C088	; tags on, before the tile loop at 9C83
109C89	C888	; tags off, after it
109398	C088	; tags on, before the tile loop at 93A2
1093A8	C888	; tags off, after it
109ACF	C088	; tags on, before the background loop at 9AD1
109ADE	C888	; tags off, after it
; Chuckie Egg, ULAX driver with the Spec256 colours.
; The point-machine commands above are those of the ULAX driver (chuckiegg.dux).
; Harry and the hens are coloured from the Spec256 file chuckie.gfx instead of the ULAX driver's own colours,
; through the Spec256 palette, as GRB555 words. Spec256 recolouring: Ville Torren.
; The ULAX driver's own palette (701000, 701010) is not used: every colour below is a direct colour word.
;
; HARRY: the ULAX driver tags a few bytes of each frame and leaves the other pixels to the colour of the last
; tag read, so some pixels had no colour of their own (the face took the hat's colour when Harry faced right;
; the eye and the arms took the colour of a ladder behind them). Here every pixel of every Harry frame has
; its own Spec256 colour (hat crown and grey brim, face, white eye, shirt, blue jeans), written as
; eight colour words per byte. The exception is the boots: Spec256 colours them with a different shade in each
; frame (and leaves some feet uncoloured, which show white), so Mr. Telmo asked for yellow rain boots: 71=FFFF00
; for the boot and 70=EFCE00 for the sole, the pixels with no pixel below them (marked "boot" on the lines).
; Two more differences from Spec256, also Mr. Telmo's choice: the SHIRT is one pale blue in every frame, between
; Spec256's two (86=DBDBDB when Harry walks, 22=9EDFD2 when he climbs: their mean is BDDDD6) and then halfway to
; white, DEEEEA; and the climbing frames get
; the grey stripe of the hat brim (76=AEAE8A on the four middle pixels of the brim row), which Spec256 gives only
; the walking frames. A hole that is closed on all sides by Harry's pixels (the pupil of the eye,
; the gaps in the body) is written as black, 0000, so nothing behind Harry shows through it.
;
; THE HENS: the ULAX driver tags bytes. A BLANK BYTE (no set pixel in the snapshot) has no Spec256 colour of
; its own. It is tagged anyway, because a tag travels with the byte when the game copies a sprite frame, and
; the pixels the game adds to the copy take the tag's colour (seen: with the ULAX tag left on them, the eating
; hen was drawn light blue). So a blank byte gets the colour of the sprite part it belongs to, taken from
; the other bytes of the same ULAX ink group; this is a choice of this file, not a value read from Spec256:
;   ink 9010 and 9011 (the hen): the most common Spec256 colour of the group, 71=FFFF00 and 69=E3A200.
;   The hens' colours are palette-index words (palette entries 1010 and up, listed at the end of this file), like
;   the ULAX driver's own, and not direct colour words: in tests the hen routine, which ORs its pixels onto the
;   screen under an ink / paper mask, lost the head of the hen eating towards the right when it was given direct
;   colours. The bytes the ULAX driver tags 8000 or 8001, which keep the plain colours of the Spectrum (the food
;   baked into the eating frames of the hen), are coloured here too, yellow, as Spec256 has them.
;
; Each line below sets the 8 pixels of one byte at the game address 80xxxx (the leftmost pixel first).
;   FFFC ink 8000      colour tag: the pixel after FFFC is the ink of the byte's set bits, the next is the
;                      paper (8000 = the Spectrum paper colour). 8001 = the Spectrum ink colour.
;   eight words        one colour per pixel: a colour word for a set bit, 8000 for a clear bit.
; The comment on each line gives the Spec256 palette index and its RGB.
;
; Sprites. Every sprite frame is 16 rows of 2 bytes (16 x 16 pixels, 32 bytes) in the game's RAM; the lines
; below give the address of a byte, its frame, row and column. The names come from the shape of the
; bitmaps in chuckiegg.sna and are inferred, not taken from any document:
;   8DF0-8EEF  Harry the farmer, 8 frames          8F90-8FEF  Harry the farmer, 4 frames (arms out)
;   9010-910F  the hen, 8 frames
; Not coloured by this driver: the text; it keeps the Spectrum attribute colours. The corn pile, the egg, the
; ladders, the big duck and its cage and the bricks (end of this file) are not coloured by the ULAX driver
; either; they are added here.
;
;
