;
; Ladders. Tiles 1 and 2 of the tile table (84F8-8507) are the left and the right half of one ladder: placed
; side by side they make a block 16 pixels wide and 8 rows high that the game repeats downwards. Pixels are
; numbered 0-15 across the block (tile 1 is 0-7, tile 2 is 8-15). The two rails are at 2-3 and 12-13, a rung
; fills rows 3-4 between them (pixels 4-11), so the ladder is 12 pixels wide.
; chuckie.gfx colours it with three Spec256 browns: 37=D29E7D (light), 36=BA8259 (one rung pixel) and
; 35=A66539 (dark). Mr. Telmo asked for the tones of a wooden ladder picture (803c425187a6bd8.png, 17 browns)
; instead. The picture is bigger than the ladder, so only its tones are used, laid out by role:
;   left pixel of each rail (2, 12), by row   C7975F B38854 BD8F5A C4945E, repeating every 4 rows
;   right pixel of each rail (3, 13), by row  695137 5E4730 634B31, repeating every 3 rows
;   top row of a rung (row 3)                 9E784C at pixel 4, then B38854 BD8F5A C7975F BD8F5A repeating
;   bottom row of a rung (row 4)              634B31 for pixels 4-7, 80613D for pixels 8-11
; The Spec256 shades are only used to find which pixels are coloured (index 0 stays 8000).
; To get the plain Spec256 colours back, use D29E7D, BA8259 and A66539 by the Spec256 index on each line.
; Ladder bitmap (set bits), one byte per line, then the colour words:
