;
; THE BRICKS (platforms), whose colours and pattern are different on each level. The brick is tile 5 of the tile
; table (84F0 + 8 * 5 = 8518), used for every platform on every level: 8 pixels wide, rows 0-4 are the brick.
; chuckie.gfx colours it with 66=BA3100 (red brick) where the bitmap has a set bit and 76=AEAE8A (mortar) where
; it has a clear bit; the game's own pattern is brick / fill / brick / fill / brick, a 'fill' row being all mortar.
; Here no pixel has a fixed colour. Each pixel holds a code FFF0 to FFF7, a slot of the translation register T
; (FFF0 is T[7], FFF1 is T[6], ... FFF7 is T[0]), and the tile loops read it through T, so a level chooses its
; colours and its pattern by what it puts in the slots. A level has a pattern, a colour for every pixel in each of
; three patterns, and a pixel's slot is picked from the three:
;   std    the game's own pattern, brick B and mortar M
;   rows3  level 6: rows a a fill b b, brick B and mortar M
;   straw  level 8: Y straw yellow, G gold, O orange, D dark orange
; The tile has only 8 slots, so the three patterns must agree on which pixels share a slot. The slots, as
; (std, rows3, straw) classes:
;   FFF0  std B, rows3 B, straw Y
;   FFF1  std B, rows3 B, straw G
;   FFF2  std M, rows3 M, straw Y
;   FFF3  std M, rows3 B, straw Y
;   FFF4  std B, rows3 M, straw O
;   FFF5  std M, rows3 M, straw O
;   FFF6  std M, rows3 B, straw O
;   FFF7  std B, rows3 B, straw D
; A level with the std pattern gives every slot the brick or mortar colour of its std class, one with the rows3
; pattern that of its rows3 class, and the straw level its own colour for each slot.
; T is loaded from the pixels of the LAST byte of the level's map block (B110 + level * 2A0 + 29F; the map blocks
; of levels 1 to 8 are at B3B0, B650, B8F0, BB90, BE30, C0D0, C370 and C610; later levels repeat them). Its eight
; pixels, leftmost first, are T[0] to T[7], that is the slots FFF7 down to FFF0.
; Plain means ZXInk (8001) for the brick and ZXPaper (8000) for the mortar: the bricks look as they do without a
; driver, the colour the game gives the platforms. Level 5 follows the MSX version (a video screenshot of it): muted
; green bricks, 4C8050, and pale mint mortar, 91B693, the two colours of its platforms (the game's own green was
; too strong). They are plain RGB, not Spec256 palette colours. Level 6 is an experiment with the colours of level
; 2 and the row order a / a / fill / b / b: brick row a (gap at pixel 5) twice, a fill row, brick row b (gap at pixel 1)
; twice. Level 3 follows the C64 version (a video screenshot of it): grey bricks, 6C6C6C, the grey the C64 uses
; (the platform lines in the picture sample at 6C6C6C to 757575), and black mortar, the plain paper. Level 8 follows
; the platforms of a remake (a video screenshot of it): a straw yellow fringe on top over gold, then orange, with
; the colours read from the picture, DFCF65, C9AF4A, A47F34, A55930 and 8E5325. Level 7 has the rows pattern of level
; 6 in dark grey bricks, 82=616161, and light grey mortar, 85=BABABA, two of the Spec256 greys. Level 4 is the look of
; level 5 turned from green to blue (same saturation and lightness, hue 0.60): bricks 4C6180 and mortar 91A0B6.
; Only level 1 is plain.
; Brick bitmap (set bits), one byte per line:
; 8518 #####.##
; 8519 ........
; 851A #.######
; 851B ........
; 851C ###.####
